YMXR License
============

YMXR is a chiptune format for the Atari ST: one encoding of the YMXS tune
data structure in a DTX table, and a player of it. This repository contains
the specification and the documents around it (doc/), the two conformance
kits (doc/conformance/ and doc/conformance-binaries/), the 68000 player with
the SNDH core and the program stub combined around it (68k/), the tools in
Java (src/main/java/org/ymxr) and in Go (go/), and the rigs, the tests and
the scripts.

The format may be implemented freely. doc/SPEC.md and doc/BINARIES.md are the
contract, and an independent writer, player or reader owes only the
acknowledgement below.

1. THE DOCUMENTS under doc/ and this file are Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van
   Dalen.

2. THE PLAYER, THE TOOLS AND THE TESTS can be used freely within your own
   programs, for any platform, even for commercial releases. The only
   condition is that you must indicate somehow in your documentation that you
   have used YMXR, and that you have used ZX1 through ST4 through DTX where
   what you ship contains a DTX image (see the attribution below). They are
   everything under 68k/, src/, go/, bin/, ym/ and release/, and the
   executables and the 68000 binaries a release carries.

The tunes under ym/test and in the two conformance kits are test input.
The published music in them belongs to its composers and is outside this
licence.

Attribution
-----------

* YMXR, its specification, its player, its tools and its tests are Copyright
  (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen; written by Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code)
  under Robbert van Dalen's direction.
* DTX, https://github.com/odipar/DTX, Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen,
  is the table format a tune is laid out in. This repository depends on DTX
  rather than copying it: the Maven artifact, and the Go module
  github.com/odipar/dtx/go. A release built here carries DTX's twenty-two
  68000 images, and each contains an ST4 decoder, so a program written with
  these tools uses ZX1 through ST4 through DTX, and your documentation
  indicates that in the same way.
* ST4, https://github.com/odipar/ST4, Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen,
  packs a DTX2 column; its compressor is derived from the ZX1 data
  compression format and algorithm, designed and implemented by Einar Saukas
  (Copyright (c) 2021), with thanks to introspec/spke. No source of either is
  kept here, so the BSD 3-Clause License that covers that compressor stands
  in ST4's and DTX's own licence files rather than in this one; a decoder in
  a DTX image is free to use on the acknowledgement above.
* YMXS, https://github.com/odipar/YMXS, Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen,
  is the tune data structure this format encodes, and every conversion here
  passes through it: the Maven artifact, and the Go module
  github.com/odipar/ymxs/go. No source of it is kept here.
* YMX, https://github.com/odipar/YMX, Copyright (c) 2026 Robbert van Dalen,
  is the family this repository belongs to: a design document defining how
  YMXS, YMXR, DTX and ST4 fit together. It was a format and a player until
  0.10.1, which YMXR replaces. No source of it is kept here.
* The YM5 and YM6 register-dump formats are by Arnaud Carré
  (Leonard/Oxygene); ym-to-ymxr reads them. The LHA depacker in
  src/main/java/org/ymxr/Lha.java and go/ym/dump.go is carried from YMX, a
  port of the ST-Sound library's LZH code by Arnaud Carré,
  itself based on LZH code by Haruhiko Okumura (1991) and Kerwin F. Medina
  (1996). It carries both notices below.
* SNDH is the Atari ST scene's shared music container; ymxr-sndh and
  ymxs-to-sndh write files of that structure (doc/BINARIES.md 3).

Used by the build and the tests, not distributed
------------------------------------------------

* rmac, the 68000 assembler, assembles the eight cores and the stub.
* Hatari, with a TOS or EmuTOS image, runs the harness on an emulated
  machine.
* The Unicorn engine (GPL-2.0) is loaded at run time by the emulator rig from
  the machine's own installed copy; no part of it is kept or distributed
  here.
* Maven pulls DTX, YMXS, JSpecify, JUnit, Error Prone and NullAway for the
  Java build, and the Go toolchain builds go/. No copy of any of them is kept
  here.

ST-Sound notices (the LHA depacker)
-----------------------------------

The LHA depacker in src/main/java/org/ymxr/Lha.java and go/ym/dump.go is a
port of StSoundLibrary/LZH/LzhLib.cpp from ST-Sound, which carries both
notices below.

